TEACHING=heading('Academic experience','Teaching','University-level mathematics instruction and problem-solving mentorship.')+f'''<section class="section-tight"><div class="shell two-col"><div><p class="kicker">Positions</p><div class="timeline"><div class="timeline-item"><span class="when">2026–present</span><h3>Graduate Teaching Assistant</h3><p>North Carolina State University</p></div><div class="timeline-item"><span class="when">2024–2026</span><h3>Lecturer</h3><p>Northern Arizona University · Calculus I, Calculus II, Introduction to Mathematical Reasoning</p></div><div class="timeline-item"><span class="when">2023–2024</span><h3>Visiting Lecturer</h3><p>Northern Arizona University · Calculus I and Finite Mathematics with Calculus</p></div><div class="timeline-item"><span class="when">2021–2023</span><h3>Graduate Teaching Assistant / Instructor</h3><p>Northern Arizona University · Algebra for Precalculus, Precalculus, and Finite Mathematics with Calculus</p></div></div></div><div><div class="content-card"><p class="kicker">Program development</p><h2 style="font-size:26px;margin:0 0 14px">Putnam preparation at NAU</h2><p>I organized a collaborative Putnam preparation program for undergraduate students. Participation grew from three to seventeen students, and the program was later approved as a one-credit course.</p><p>The sessions emphasized independent problem solving, discussing proofs, and presenting different approaches.</p></div></div></div></section>'''

ABOUT=heading('Background','About','Education, research background, and technical experience.')+f'''<section class="section-tight"><div class="shell two-col"><div class="body-copy"><p class="lead">I am a mathematics PhD student at North Carolina State University.</p><p>I studied mathematics and physics at Northern Arizona University, where I later completed an MS in mathematics. My background includes computational modeling, mathematical research, and university teaching.</p><p>For my master's thesis, I used persistent homology and machine learning to study parameter estimation from numerical solutions of a partial differential equation. Earlier, I studied nonlinear dynamics in a double-pendulum model. I have also coauthored research on the algebraic theory of impartial combinatorial games.</p><p>My current interests include scientific machine learning, PDEs, dynamical systems, and topology. I am interested in computational research and scientific software roles as well as academic collaboration.</p><div class="cta-row"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="mailto:{EMAIL}">Email me {icon('arrow')}</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="assets/docs/resume.pdf">Resume (PDF) {icon('down')}</a></div></div><div><div class="content-card"><p class="kicker">Education</p><div class="timeline"><div class="timeline-item"><span class="when">2026–present</span><h3>PhD, Mathematics</h3><p>North Carolina State University</p></div><div class="timeline-item"><span class="when">2023</span><h3>MS, Mathematics</h3><p>Northern Arizona University</p></div><div class="timeline-item"><span class="when">2021</span><h3>BS, Mathematics · BS, Physics</h3><p>Northern Arizona University</p></div></div></div><div class="content-card" style="margin-top:18px"><p class="kicker">Selected tools</p><div class="skill-cloud">{''.join('<span class="tag">'+x+'</span>' for x in ['Python','NumPy / SciPy','scikit-learn','PyTorch','TensorFlow','MATLAB','Persistent homology','GUDHI','Numerical methods'])}</div></div></div></div></section>'''

TDA=heading('Master’s thesis · 2023','Topological data analysis for PDE parameter estimation','Northern Arizona University',True)+f'''<section class="section-tight"><div class="shell detail-layout"><article class="body-copy"><p class="lead">I investigated the use of persistent homology and machine learning to infer parameters from spatial patterns generated by a nonlinear partial differential equation.</p><h2>Problem</h2><p>The thesis considers numerically generated surfaces from an anisotropic Kuramoto–Sivashinsky-type PDE. The aim was to distinguish the parameter values used to generate them by extracting features from the resulting patterns.</p><h2>Methods</h2><p>The dataset contained <strong>850 surfaces of size 200 × 200</strong> spanning <strong>17 parameter classes</strong>, with 50 initial conditions per class. I computed cubical persistent homology, constructed representations including persistence images, and compared classification methods.</p><p>Model evaluation included five-fold cross-validation, hyperparameter searches, and comparisons with models trained directly on the original surfaces. Experiments used Python and computing resources at Northern Arizona University.</p><h2>Results</h2><p>In the thesis experiments, a random-forest classifier using persistence images achieved <strong>79.96% mean five-fold cross-validation accuracy</strong>. Results varied by classifier and feature representation; these figures describe this specific simulated dataset.</p><h2>Code</h2><p><a href="https://github.com/mishbolt/TDA-Thesis" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TDA-Thesis repository ↗</a></p></article><aside class="aside-panel"><h2>Project details</h2><dl><dt>Type</dt><dd>Master’s thesis</dd><dt>Institution</dt><dd>Northern Arizona University</dd><dt>Advisor</dt><dd>Rachel Neville</dd><dt>Completed</dt><dd>2023</dd><dt>Methods</dt><dd>Cubical persistence, persistence images, random forests, kNN, SVM, cross-validation</dd></dl><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="../assets/docs/cv.pdf">Full CV ↓</a></aside></div></section>'''

PENDULUM=heading('Undergraduate research · 2020–2021','Nonlinear dynamics of a double pendulum','Northern Arizona University',True)+f'''<section class="section-tight"><div class="shell detail-layout"><article class="body-copy"><p class="lead">An undergraduate research project on numerical and analytical methods for a nonlinear Hamiltonian system.</p><h2>Equations and normal modes</h2><p>I derived the equations using Lagrangian and Hamiltonian mechanics and studied normal modes in the small-oscillation regime. Numerical integration in MATLAB provided comparisons with the analytical approximations.</p><h2>Poincaré sections</h2><p>Poincaré sections reduce the visualization of a four-dimensional phase space to a lower-dimensional view. I used them to examine regular trajectories, resonances, and transitions to chaotic behavior as initial conditions and energy changed.</p><figure class="figure"><img src="../assets/images/poincare-sections.webp" alt="Poincaré sections from the author's double-pendulum research report" width="782" height="351" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Selected Poincaré sections from the research report.</figcaption></figure><h2>Lyapunov exponents and perturbation methods</h2><p>I investigated Lyapunov spectra using variational equations and repeated orthonormalization, and studied Poincaré–Lindstedt approximations and numerical approaches to periodic orbits.</p><h2>Code</h2><p><a href="https://github.com/mishbolt/MATLAB-Double-Pendulum" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MATLAB-Double-Pendulum repository ↗</a></p></article><aside class="aside-panel"><h2>Project details</h2><dl><dt>Type</dt><dd>Undergraduate research</dd><dt>Institution</dt><dd>Northern Arizona University</dd><dt>Dates</dt><dd>2020–2021</dd><dt>Methods</dt><dd>Numerical integration, Hamiltonian dynamics, perturbation theory, Lyapunov spectra</dd></dl><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="../projects.html">Projects ↗</a></aside></div></section>'''

GAMES=heading('Journal article · 2025','Isomorphism theorems for impartial combinatorial games','Joint work with Dana C. Ernst and Nándor Sieben',True)+f'''<section class="section-tight"><div class="shell detail-layout"><article class="body-copy"><p class="lead">This work studies algebraic structures associated with impartial combinatorial games and their representations as directed graphs.</p><h2>Framework</h2><p>We use <strong>optiongraphs</strong> and <strong>option-preserving maps</strong> to describe games in a setting that permits cycles and infinite play. The framework provides a language for congruences and quotient constructions.</p><h2>Results</h2><p>The paper establishes analogues of the isomorphism theorems familiar from algebra and studies their consequences for optiongraphs. The approach also addresses game-theoretic quantities preserved under suitable maps.</p><h2>Publication</h2><p>M. Baltushkin, D. C. Ernst, and N. Sieben, “Isomorphism theorems for impartial combinatorial games,” <em>Discrete Mathematics Letters</em> <strong>16</strong> (2025), 59–66.</p><p><a href="{DOI}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Published article ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.dmlett.com/archive/v16/DML25_v16_pp59-66.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PDF ↗</a></p></article><aside class="aside-panel"><h2>Citation</h2><dl><dt>Authors</dt><dd>M. Baltushkin, D. C. Ernst, N. Sieben</dd><dt>Journal</dt><dd>Discrete Mathematics Letters</dd><dt>Volume / pages</dt><dd>16 (2025), 59–66</dd><dt>DOI</dt><dd><a href="{DOI}">10.47443/dml.2025.071 ↗</a></dd></dl></aside></div></section>'''

